Z||000009||fierofreek2001||04-12-2001||10:12 PM||||gotta question... probably pretty stupid sounding to most people, but here goes... how do u hook up more than one amp ...?   i have 2 amps i want to hook up.. one for 2 subs, and the other amp that is powering 6x9s right now... and i have no idea how to do that...   im an amatuer can u tell? i have a power wire already routed under the computer and all, do i need to have another power wire or what?<BR>thanks<BR>steve||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||04-12-2001||10:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierofreek2001:<BR><B>gotta question... probably pretty stupid sounding to most people, but here goes... how do u hook up more than one amp ...?   i have 2 amps i want to hook up.. one for 2 subs, and the other amp that is powering 6x9s right now... and i have no idea how to do that...   im an amatuer can u tell? i have a power wire already routed under the computer and all, do i need to have another power wire or what?<BR>thanks<BR>steve</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Depending on the size of the power wire, you can use the same wire for the second amp. I am using an 8 gauge wire from the battery to both amps, the crossover, and the processor.<BR>8 gauge is good for up to 500W, 6 gauge is good for up to 800W and 4 gauge is good up to 1500W. It is VERY important to use the same gauge wire for the ground of the amps too. Mine has an 8 guage that splits into 2 10 guage for the amps. The crossover and processor use very low current so I tap off each amp for their power. As for input to the amps, there are many electronic 2-way crossovers available. Stereo input goes in and you get high freqs out one and sub freqs out the other. The black box in my pics is the crossover. The white is the processor.<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||04-12-2001||11:14 PM||||Fierofreek: You can buy a crossover, which works awesome, but you have a couple more options: you can use a RCA splitter (be careful. Needs to be decent) if your deck only has one set of preouts, run another RCA cable entirely (now have fade control, if you need it) or if the amp is equiped with a pass-through (like my Rockford  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) you can use another short RCA.<BR>As for power, if you are running 8AWG, you'll be fine. 8AWG is good to 30amp draw @ 15 feet w/ &lt;3% Voltage drop, which is MORE than enough. I don't believe that you need such thick wires for stereo installs, although, if you are going for show, do look good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> You do not gain any significant advantage by going with bigger than 2AWG wire for any system that draws less than 100amps.<BR>10AWG will more than likely easily accomidate your needs, if that's what you have, as it's good to 20amp draw @ 15ft w/ &lt;3%Voltage drop., or 60amp @ 10% V-drop.||||reg||6
Z||000012||AkursedX||04-12-2001||11:24 PM||||And I though I had a nice stereo setup!! That looks great and you'll definietly be getting some high quality sound out of all of that.  Looks like a very professional install too.||||reg||14
Z||000013||Oreif||04-12-2001||11:43 PM||||To calculate what size wire you need, Add the total watts the amps put out at max. Divide this by 14.4 and the answer is the max amps drawn. You want the wire and fuse to be at least 20% higher. If you use "just the right size" the wire will heat up. Even slightly heating up will add resistance to the wire and cause current limiting. My system is 450W max. So I could draw 31.25 amps Max. <BR>Since an 8 gauge fine strand wire can handle 45 amps (75% of max current of an 8 gauge wire), I am using the 8 AWG with a 40 amp fuse. It is obvious that I will in reality never see the max current draw but I will never have to worry about the power line getting hot. Also if a power wire starts to current limit due to heat/resistance, it will be more suseptable at picking up engine noise and cause the music to sound distorted.<BR>10 gauge is good for one amp but for 2 amps I strongly urge you to use an 8 gauge. (better safe than sorry) <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
